1. What is JDBC? Why is it used?
JDBC stands for Java Database Connectivity.
It is a standard Java API used to connect a Java application with a database application.
It is used to execute SQL queries and access data stored in databases from Java programs.
2. What are the problems of a file system that led to the use of databases?
	The problems of a file system are:
	Data is stored in plain files.
	There is no efficient query processing and access is manual.
	Security provided is very basic.
	Redundant data can be present and consistency is less.
	Concurrency is difficult to manage because there is no built-in control.
	Backup and recovery are manual and time-consuming.
	It is hard to manage large data and not suitable for complex transactions.


3. Difference between File System and Database?
File System stores data in plain files, whereas Database stores data in structured tables.
File System has no efficient query processing, whereas Database supports SQL queries.
File System provides less security, whereas Database provides strong security.
File System allows data redundancy, whereas Database avoids redundancy and ensures consistency.
File System has no built-in concurrency control, whereas Database supports safe multi-user access.
Backup and recovery are manual in File System, but automated in Database.

4. What is a JDBC Driver? Why is it required?
A JDBC Driver is software that helps a Java application communicate with a database.
It is required because JDBC needs a database-specific driver to convert Java statements into a database-understandable language.

5. Is JDBC API dependent on a database or driver? Explain.
JDBC API is independent of any database or driver.
However, to communicate with a database, a specific JDBC Driver must be integrated into the Java application.

6. What is JDBC Driver?
	JDBC Driver is a software component that enables java application to interact with the database. There are   4 types of JDBC drivers:
	JDBC-ODBC bridge driver
	Native-API driver (partially java driver)
	Network Protocol driver (fully java driver)
	Thin driver (fully java driver)
JDBC-ODBC bridge driver
	The JDBC-ODBC bridge driver uses ODBC driver to connect to the database. 
	The JDBC-ODBC bridge driver converts JDBC method calls into the ODBC functioncalls. 
	This is now discouraged because of thin driver.
	Oracle does not support the JDBC-ODBC Bridge from Java 8.Oracle recommends that you use JDBC drivers provided by the vendor of your database instead of the JDBC-ODBC Bridge.


2. Native-API driver
The Native API driver uses the client-side libraries of the database.
 The driver converts JDBC method calls into native calls of the database API. It is not written entirely in java.
3. Network Protocol driver
The Network Protocol driver uses middleware (application server) that converts
JDBC calls directly or indirectly into the vendor-specific database protocol. It is fully written in java.
4. Thin driver
The thin driver converts JDBC calls directly into the vendor-specific database
protocol. That is why it is known as thin driver. It is fully written in Java language.



